# 03-code-feature-folder-file-structure.mdc - Feature Folder, File, and Class Responsibility Rules

## Scope

- MUST treat one feature as the smallest reusable responsibility unit.
- MUST keep all files for one feature under one feature folder unless the framework requires a different location.
- MUST treat the Unity examples as reference structures that also apply to non-Unity projects after adapting names, extensions, and platform concepts.
- MUST organize folders, files, and classes around SOLID principles, especially single responsibility, interface segregation, and dependency inversion.
- MUST write code that anyone can understand.
- MUST keep design clear and avoid structures that block extension.
- MUST keep behavior stable and reproducible.
- MUST keep operation consistent enough for handoff.

## SOLID Principles

- MUST follow Single Responsibility Principle: one class has one responsibility.
- MUST follow Open/Closed Principle: code is open for extension and closed for unnecessary modification.
- MUST follow Liskov Substitution Principle: derived classes remain compatible with their base class.
- MUST follow Interface Segregation Principle: avoid large interfaces that force unused methods.
- MUST follow Dependency Inversion Principle: depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations.

## Custom Structural Principles

- MUST use one folder per feature to close responsibility within the feature.
- MUST align folder responsibility with class responsibility.
- MUST centralize feature entry and dependency control through a single manager.
- MUST separate responsibilities such as display, presentation effects, logic, and data.
- MUST separate display, presentation effects, business or game logic, and data responsibilities.
- MUST keep dependency direction one-way.
- MUST depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations when decoupling is required.
- SHOULD isolate cross-feature common behavior into an independent common layer.

## Standard Feature Classes

- MUST keep one feature entry point, usually `FeatureManager`.
- MUST use `FeatureManager` for initialization, dependency resolution, and update-loop management.
- MUST NOT create multiple independent managers for one feature.
- MUST introduce one higher-level coordinator if multiple managers are unavoidable.
- MUST use `FeatureController` for input interpretation, service coordination, and view state control.
- MUST use `FeatureView` for UI or display control.
- MUST use `FeatureService` for game logic or business logic.
- MUST use `FeatureData` for settings, value objects, and data structures.
- SHOULD use this baseline structure for one feature:

```text
FeatureName/
├─ FeatureManager.cs
├─ FeatureController.cs
├─ FeatureView.cs
├─ FeatureService.cs
└─ FeatureData.cs
```

## Game Feature Structure

- SHOULD split game features into UI and scene control when the feature controls both UI and visual scene elements.
- SHOULD treat the non-UI scene element as the visual or presentation part added to UI control.
- SHOULD use `FeatureUIController`, `FeatureUIView`, `FeatureUIService`, and `FeatureUIData` for UI concerns.
- SHOULD use `FeatureSceneController`, `FeatureSceneView`, `FeatureSceneService`, and `FeatureSceneData` for scene concerns.
- MAY add `FeatureCameraController`, `FeatureCharacterController`, or `FeatureBackgroundController` only for scene elements owned by the feature.
- MAY add `FeatureEffectController` or `FeatureSoundController` only when the feature owns unique effect or sound behavior.
- SHOULD avoid feature-specific effect or sound controllers when common game systems already own those concerns.
- SHOULD use this basic game feature structure:

```text
FeatureName/
├─ FeatureManager.cs
├─ FeatureUIController.cs
│  ├─ FeatureUIView.cs
│  ├─ FeatureUIService.cs
│  └─ FeatureUIData.cs
└─ FeatureSceneController.cs
   ├─ FeatureSceneView.cs
   ├─ FeatureSceneService.cs
   └─ FeatureSceneData.cs
```

- SHOULD use this expanded scene structure when a game feature maps to one scene or screen:

```text
FeatureName/
├─ FeatureManager.cs
│  ├─ FeatureUIController.cs
│  │  ├─ FeatureUIView.cs
│  │  ├─ FeatureUIService.cs
│  │  └─ FeatureUIData.cs
│  ├─ FeatureSceneController.cs
│  │  ├─ FeatureSceneView.cs
│  │  ├─ FeatureSceneService.cs
│  │  ├─ FeatureSceneData.cs
│  │  ├─ FeatureCameraController.cs
│  │  ├─ FeatureCharacterController.cs
│  │  ├─ FeatureBackgroundController.cs
│  │  ├─ FeatureEffectController.cs
│  │  └─ FeatureSoundController.cs
│  └─ FeatureService.cs / FeatureData.cs
```

- MUST keep only one manager as the feature entry point.
- MUST add a higher-level coordinator when multiple manager-like classes are unavoidable.
- MAY replace character, background, camera, effect, and sound controllers with equivalents for the actual controlled object, such as cards or Live2D.
- SHOULD omit effect and sound controllers when common systems own effects or sound.

## Recommended Layout

```text
FeatureName/
├─ FeatureManager.cs
├─ FeatureService.cs
├─ FeatureData.cs
└─ FeatureController/
   ├─ FeatureUI/
   │  ├─ FeatureUIController.cs
   │  ├─ FeatureUIView.cs
   │  ├─ FeatureUIService.cs
   │  └─ FeatureUIData.cs
   └─ FeatureScene/
      ├─ FeatureSceneController.cs
      ├─ FeatureSceneView.cs
      ├─ FeatureSceneService.cs
      ├─ FeatureSceneData.cs
      ├─ FeatureCameraController.cs
      ├─ FeatureCharacterController.cs
      ├─ FeatureBackgroundController.cs
      ├─ FeatureEffectController.cs
      └─ FeatureSoundController.cs
```
